1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,820
It's only seven blocks long, but it may be the most important street on Earth.

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:11,600
Wall Street. It's fueled some of the greatest economic expansions ever known.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:14,960
It's plunged millions into poverty and ruin.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:23,940
Now the thrill, the terror, the technique, and the technology of the Stock Exchange on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:45,651 --> 00:00:51,180
Every business day, more than a billion shares change hands on America's major stock exchanges.

6
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,180
Nearly $90 million worth of stock changes hands every minute.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
I said 200 A-packs, 200 A-packs.

8
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:10,000
Brokers constantly scramble to do their clients' bidding in the battlefield of high finance.

9
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:13,000
The floor is a rather unique kind of place.

10
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:16,000
It's a place where people have known each other many years.

11
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:21,000
Each day get together to compete on behalf of clients they've never met.

12
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:26,000
A stock exchange is actually two markets in one.

13
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:33,260
First, it's a place where a new company can raise millions or billions of dollars overnight

14
00:01:33,261 --> 00:01:40,100
by offering ownership of the business to hundreds of thousands of small investors.

15
00:01:40,101 --> 00:01:44,240
These pieces of ownership are called shares of stock.

16
00:01:44,241 --> 00:01:50,240
Second, the exchange is the place where people can sell their stock to someone else whenever

17
00:01:50,241 --> 00:01:51,240
they wish.

18
00:01:51,241 --> 00:01:55,200
You could be in the airline business today, and you could be out of that, and you could

19
00:01:55,201 --> 00:01:57,749
be in the automobile business tomorrow.

20
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:00,820
And that ability to enter and exit and get paid

21
00:02:00,821 --> 00:02:06,720
back for your investment in those shares creates a great market for the initial investment.

22
00:02:06,721 --> 00:02:10,860
People are more willing to put that money up if they know that sometime in the future

23
00:02:10,861 --> 00:02:14,660
they'll be able to sell out their interest and go on to the next best thing.

24
00:02:14,661 --> 00:02:18,855
Forty percent of American families own stock in U.S.

25
00:02:18,856 --> 00:02:21,760
companies either directly or through

26
00:02:21,761 --> 00:02:25,520
mutual funds and pension plans.

27
00:02:25,521 --> 00:02:30,920
The exchange lets people know how these companies are doing by allowing a free market to place

28
00:02:30,921 --> 00:02:32,860
a value on their stock.

29
00:02:32,861 --> 00:02:37,360
Well, I like to say that a stock market is a market in information.

30
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:43,700
I mean, it uses information from outside to evaluate companies, but then the process of

31
00:02:43,701 --> 00:02:48,460
the stock market creates new information by putting a price on things that everybody in

32
00:02:48,461 --> 00:02:51,360
the country can read.

33
00:02:51,361 --> 00:02:56,840
The modern market is a tightly regulated and finely tuned institution.

34
00:02:56,841 --> 00:02:59,740
But this wasn't always the case.

35
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:13,480
Centuries ago, investing in stocks was chaos.

36
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:17,820
The roots of investment trace back to ancient Greece.

37
00:03:17,821 --> 00:03:23,460
captains offered part of their profits to those who'd share the risk of financing a trading voyage.

38
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:27,023
These were all or nothing gambles.

39
00:03:27,024 --> 00:03:31,120
A ship might return with riches or might never return at all.

40
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:39,000
The Romans sold stock as well for huge construction projects that were beyond the

41
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:41,560
means of a single businessman.

42
00:03:41,561 --> 00:03:46,340
Stockholders made tidy profits by investing in companies

43
00:03:46,341 --> 00:03:48,980
that built roads and aqueducts for the government.

44
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:55,700
Investing took a big step in the 1600s in Holland,

45
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:57,860
home of the Dutch East India Company.

46
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:01,340
Like the Greek seafarers before them,

47
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,600
the Dutch needed capital for international trade.

48
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,400
But this investment was different.

49
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,380
People bought a stake in the company, not in a single voyage.

50
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:14,560
This led to wild speculation

51
00:04:14,561 --> 00:04:18,040
because investors could sell their shares to one another over time.

52
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:23,040
The price could rise or fall on the vaguest of rumors,

53
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:26,220
spreading panic in the world's first stock exchange.

54
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:29,980
The people who lost the money were the nobles, the merchants,

55
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:34,440
a class of people who, in effect, were the money people of the time

56
00:04:34,441 --> 00:04:35,420
who can afford to speculate.

57
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:36,900
It didn't affect the economy.

58
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:38,860
There was no problem there.

59
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:42,300
And as far as the average person is concerned,

60
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:43,880
when the crash came, he might have said to himself,

61
00:04:43,881 --> 00:04:45,860
It's a good thing it happened. These people deserve what I got.

62
00:04:45,861 --> 00:04:46,740
They're a bunch of gamblers.

63
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:53,360
The reputation of stockbrokers sunk even lower during the 1700s.

64
00:04:53,361 --> 00:04:56,340
In Paris, an escaped murderer sold stock.

65
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:01,520
John Law swindled the public with worthless shares in fictitious gold mines.

66
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:10,520
In London, brokers lured investors with stories of a secret device that could turn chickens into sheep.

67
00:05:10,521 --> 00:05:18,020
The value of these stocks would skyrocket until reason took hold.

68
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:23,040
Nothing is being done, but the shares are becoming more and more valuable as time goes on.

69
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,840
Eventually, one person says, how can these shares possibly be worth 5,000 pounds?

70
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:28,740
It's ridiculous.

71
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:30,000
And he starts selling.

72
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:32,820
And then another person sells, and it goes to 4,000 pounds.

73
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:35,300
And now a panic sets in, and the whole thing falls apart.

74
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:38,660
Despite the unscrupulous brokers,

75
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:43,760
stocks helped make Europe the colonial and economic powerhouse of the world.

76
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,700
They would soon do the same for America.

77
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:01,100
Wall Street got its name when New York was a tiny colonial outpost.

78
00:06:01,101 --> 00:06:08,100
In 1653, pilgrims built a wall to keep out Indians.

79
00:06:08,101 --> 00:06:14,100
One hundred years later, the wall was gone.

80
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:22,100
But the path that ran beside it had become the heart of New York's commerce and society.

81
00:06:22,101 --> 00:06:26,100
Here was the auction block for slaves,

82
00:06:26,101 --> 00:06:29,580
and the pillory for public humiliation.

83
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:41,520
George Washington was sworn in as the first U.S. president on Wall Street in 1789.

84
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:49,480
Wall Street was also where merchants gathered beneath a buttonwood tree

85
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:55,080
to auction off stock in banks and mines, making a commission on every sale.

86
00:06:56,100 --> 00:07:04,940
This open-air market lasted until 1792, when 24 merchants signed a document known as the Buttonwood Agreement.

87
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:11,020
The pact was an effort to avoid government regulation of street auctions.

88
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:14,000
It also blocked newcomers from the business.

89
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:19,620
And a group of the auctioneers, the most powerful ones, say, you know, anyone's coming into this market.

90
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:21,560
We're losing business to these people.

91
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:26,460
So let's form an organization called the New York Stock and Exchange Board in that tavern over there.

92
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:29,460
And we'll have a door, and we'll keep these people out.

93
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:35,980
The brokers met inside the Tontine Coffeehouse for two formal auctions per day.

94
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:38,460
The public was not allowed.

95
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:43,040
If you wanted to buy or sell stock, you had to hire a broker to do it.

96
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:45,820
What it did is it did one thing.

97
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:48,440
It made the trading of stocks less public.

98
00:07:48,441 --> 00:07:52,080
so the public had less information about what was going on.

99
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:55,940
In the open auction system, where everybody could hear,

100
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,860
people knew more about what was going on.

101
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:04,780
These indoor sessions where a central auctioneer sold stocks one by one

102
00:08:04,781 --> 00:08:09,340
allowed brokers to regulate themselves to prevent fraud and abuse.

103
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:14,600
Only reputable stocks were traded, and the sales were recorded.

104
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,980
These procedures created an air of respectability.

105
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:26,540
You change something from mere commerce of hawking with wares on the street,

106
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:32,020
you sort of put it indoors and got a certain group of people who knew each other.

107
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:36,960
You're moving from a trade or just ordinary business to a profession.

108
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:43,960
During the auctions, prices were quoted in increments of one-eighth of a dollar.

109
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:49,940
a holdover from the days when people carried Spanish mill dollars and cut them into pieces of eight.

110
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:56,420
Only 30 companies were traded inside the exchange.

111
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:01,880
Banks, cargo insurance companies, construction firms that built bridges and piers.

112
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,460
But only a handful of fearless investors dared buy them.

113
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:13,560
Many people were afraid of stock because if the company went bankrupt, the stock owners could lose everything.

114
00:09:13,561 --> 00:09:19,920
So stocks were for two kinds of people, outright speculators or people who try to take control of the company.

115
00:09:21,080 --> 00:09:26,340
The struggle for respectability on Wall Street brought only limited success.

116
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:34,300
With time, the trading of less reputable stocks resumed on the street among brokers who weren't allowed to join the exchange.

117
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,380
Here, disputes were settled with fists.

118
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,840
Big changes, though, were in store for Wall Street.

119
00:09:42,841 --> 00:09:47,360
America's westward expansion would soon spark an explosion of activity.

120
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:54,080
And technology would speed up the pace of business beyond anyone's wildest dream.

121
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:02,720
In 1789, the first government bonds were traded on Wall Street,

122
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:07,180
$80 million worth to pay for the recent war against England.

123
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:11,620
The Stock Exchange will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

124
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:16,920
We now return to the Stock Exchange on Modern Marvels.

125
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:25,100
Several legends describe how the terms bull and bear found their way to Wall Street.

126
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:30,620
The most dramatic tale involves a style of bullfight in the old Wild West.

127
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:36,360
A thousand-pound grizzly would be chained to a stake in a bullfight arena.

128
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:40,280
Then a bull would charge.

129
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:45,480
The bull could win the contest by thrusting upward with its horns.

130
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:50,720
The bear could survive by wrestling the bull down, breaking its neck.

131
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:57,240
On Wall Street, bulls were brokers who expected stock prices to rise.

132
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:01,780
Bears were traders who anticipated drops in the market.

133
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:07,540
And many bears used the declines to outwit other investors.

134
00:11:07,541 --> 00:11:12,580
In those days, if you were on Wall Street, you had a dark side.

135
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:15,180
Wall Street was no place for amateurs.

136
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:18,040
There was no federal government on Wall Street.

137
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,260
The state government didn't have much to do.

138
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:22,740
The rules are very few and far between.

139
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:25,500
In other words, it was a jungle.

140
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:31,100
The Bulls included colorful figures like Hetty Green,

141
00:11:31,101 --> 00:11:37,880
whose severe appearance and miserly character earned her the nickname the Witch of Wall Street.

142
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:43,640
Green became the world's richest woman by investing in railroads.

143
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:58,140
America's railroads were among the most ambitious engineering projects in history.

144
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:06,100
Railroad stocks fueled the frenzy on Wall Street.

145
00:12:06,101 --> 00:12:12,100
Up until the railroads, corporations were little small little mom-and-pa shops.

146
00:12:12,101 --> 00:12:14,100
They didn't need a lot of capital.

147
00:12:14,101 --> 00:12:18,100
When you're building a big railroad, you need to assemble a lot of money.

148
00:12:18,101 --> 00:12:22,100
And at that point, the stock market became very important.

149
00:12:22,101 --> 00:12:29,100
With shrewd rail investments, Hedy Green amassed a fortune of $100 million.

150
00:12:29,101 --> 00:12:32,840
The equivalent of nearly $2 billion today.

151
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:36,420
Her advice to others was simple.

152
00:12:38,180 --> 00:12:40,220
There is no great secret to fortune making.

153
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:43,400
All you have to do is buy cheap and sell dear.

154
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:45,740
Act with thrift and shrewdness.

155
00:12:46,280 --> 00:12:46,980
And be persistent.

156
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:49,180
Hedy Green.

157
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:53,480
To accommodate the railroad boom,

158
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:58,120
the New York Stock Exchange moved several times in search of larger quarters.

159
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:03,980
settling in 1865 in a building near the corner of Wall and Broad.

160
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:10,860
To create a pleasant atmosphere, the building's ventilation system

161
00:13:10,861 --> 00:13:14,840
wafted perfumed air across the crowded trading floor.

162
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:25,180
To protect stock certificates, the basement housed hundreds of vaults.

163
00:13:25,181 --> 00:13:33,880
by 1870 the old system of auctioning stocks gave way to a free-for-all

164
00:13:33,881 --> 00:13:39,160
brokers roamed the floor making deals throughout the day

165
00:13:39,161 --> 00:13:46,360
in this crowd were ruthless marauders known as wall street bears

166
00:13:46,361 --> 00:13:54,120
the markets in those days particularly the stock market was an arena for the gunslingers

167
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:57,560
the takeover artists, the robber barons.

168
00:13:58,020 --> 00:13:59,260
This was their playground,

169
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:03,920
and that's where they ran prices of stocks up and down

170
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:07,400
to suit whatever shenanigans they were trying to pull at the time.

171
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:12,080
The most notorious bear was a former store clerk

172
00:14:12,081 --> 00:14:14,880
who became the market's most detested raider.

173
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:19,540
Jay Gould was one of the most famous speculators in Wall Street.

174
00:14:19,541 --> 00:14:23,160
He was called the Mephistopheles, sort of the devil of Wall Street,

175
00:14:23,161 --> 00:14:26,640
because he was always looking out for number one

176
00:14:26,641 --> 00:14:29,300
and not really thinking so much about companies.

177
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:30,540
That's what people say.

178
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:34,480
I think he was also interested in building up the value of some companies.

179
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:35,760
But he was a manipulator.

180
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:41,760
Gould earned his seat among the kings of Wall Street

181
00:14:41,761 --> 00:14:43,400
by mastering the short sale,

182
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:47,340
a technique to make money when a stock went down in price.

183
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:51,280
Gould would persuade unwitting investors

184
00:14:51,281 --> 00:14:54,200
to loan him their shares at a small rate of interest.

185
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:57,360
He would immediately sell them at the exchange.

186
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:02,120
Gould then published vicious rumors about the company

187
00:15:02,121 --> 00:15:04,080
in a newspaper he owned

188
00:15:04,081 --> 00:15:06,440
in order to drive its stock price down.

189
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:09,780
He bought the shares back at the lower price

190
00:15:09,781 --> 00:15:11,340
and returned them to their owner.

191
00:15:12,220 --> 00:15:15,780
His profit was the difference between the price he received when selling

192
00:15:15,781 --> 00:15:18,840
and the price he later paid to buy the stock back.

193
00:15:18,841 --> 00:15:26,720
The fact that a company and its shareholders might be ruined in the process seemed of no concern to Gould.

194
00:15:31,020 --> 00:15:35,540
The bulls and bears made Wall Street a rambunctious place.

195
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:41,280
The street itself remained a busy marketplace for the so-called curbstone brokers

196
00:15:41,281 --> 00:15:45,640
who continued to trade the stocks of smaller companies out of doors

197
00:15:45,641 --> 00:15:50,620
until they formed the American Stock Exchange in the 1920s.

198
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:56,380
The bull and bear struggle also made Wall Street dangerous.

199
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:02,560
Every 20 years, the street would fill with investors panicked over a crash in prices.

200
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:07,380
Sometimes the exchange was forced to close for days.

201
00:16:07,381 --> 00:16:16,800
The outbreak of World War I sparked a panic that closed the market for more than four months

202
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:31,660
In 1832, a simple device revolutionized the stock exchange

203
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:37,720
The Telegraph allowed news from distant cities to flash across the United States.

204
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:45,520
In the world of finance where information is power, the Telegraph was a godsend.

205
00:16:47,760 --> 00:16:53,720
The Telegraph's inventor, Samuel Morse, opened a demonstration office near the stock exchange,

206
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:57,460
charging brokers 25 cents to see his invention.

207
00:16:57,461 --> 00:17:04,460
Soon after, Wall Street was a tangle of telegraph wires.

208
00:17:04,461 --> 00:17:08,460
The telegraph turned New York into America's financial capital

209
00:17:08,461 --> 00:17:12,460
by eliminating the need for regional markets in other cities.

210
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:19,460
The telegraph makes it possible for New York to become a central marketplace.

211
00:17:19,461 --> 00:17:22,460
At one time we had dozens of stock exchanges all over the country.

212
00:17:22,461 --> 00:17:26,460
There was a stock exchange in Albany, there was a stock exchange in Buffalo.

213
00:17:26,461 --> 00:17:30,540
But once the telegraph goes to Albany and Buffalo from New York, they disappear.

214
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:34,580
The stock ticker was the next innovation.

215
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:40,860
Created in 1867, the ticker printed telegraph signals onto a narrow paper tape.

216
00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:46,540
The ticker brought up-to-the-minute prices to brokers throughout the nation.

217
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:52,880
For the first time, people outside the exchange could tell what was going on.

218
00:17:52,881 --> 00:17:57,660
July 8th, 1889

219
00:17:57,661 --> 00:17:59,560
another milestone

220
00:17:59,561 --> 00:18:02,880
issue number one of the Wall Street Journal

221
00:18:02,881 --> 00:18:04,920
the price, two cents

222
00:18:04,921 --> 00:18:09,420
Charles Dow and Edward Jones

223
00:18:09,421 --> 00:18:11,960
raised the standard of financial journalism

224
00:18:11,961 --> 00:18:13,280
with their new publication

225
00:18:13,281 --> 00:18:17,420
the most popular feature

226
00:18:17,421 --> 00:18:19,980
was the daily index of 12 stocks

227
00:18:19,981 --> 00:18:23,140
known as the Dow Jones Industrial Average.

228
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:28,720
By analyzing the performance of key companies

229
00:18:28,721 --> 00:18:32,000
such as American Sugar, U.S. Rubber, and General Electric,

230
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:34,940
the Dow became a stock market barometer.

231
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:40,700
It made sense out of what was perceived as chaos.

232
00:18:41,260 --> 00:18:44,640
In the daily jumble of up an eighth and down a quarter,

233
00:18:44,641 --> 00:18:49,460
it was hard to tell whether stock prices generally were moving up or down.

234
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:55,640
With the industrial average, you could keep your eye on the long-term trends

235
00:18:55,641 --> 00:18:59,220
and not be confused by the short-term static.

236
00:19:00,060 --> 00:19:05,040
The ticker, the telegraph, and the Wall Street Journal were powerful tools

237
00:19:05,041 --> 00:19:08,400
that opened the world of stocks to the general public.

238
00:19:08,401 --> 00:19:21,200
The most powerful man in America was the son of a prominent banker

239
00:19:21,201 --> 00:19:26,700
who made his own mark in history as the undisputed king of corporate mergers.

240
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:29,600
His name, J.P. Morgan.

241
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:36,380
Morgan combined hundreds of independent railroads and factories

242
00:19:36,381 --> 00:19:38,920
into coast-to-coast monopolies.

243
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:43,640
Morgan's greatest achievement came in 1901,

244
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:47,440
when nine companies joined forces to become U.S. Steel.

245
00:19:48,660 --> 00:19:52,380
It was the world's first billion-dollar corporation.

246
00:19:52,381 --> 00:19:58,380
With stocks so valuable, it boosted the Dow Jones average by 500%.

247
00:19:58,381 --> 00:19:59,081
J.P.

248
00:19:59,082 --> 00:20:09,620
Morgan was a stern, autocratic man who never gambled and based his decisions on business fundamentals.

249
00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:16,520
To protect his customers, Morgan insisted on a role in managing every company he created.

250
00:20:17,740 --> 00:20:26,700
If he sold you a security, a stock in a railroad company, if the railroad company got into trouble, he would move in and try to improve the situation.

251
00:20:26,701 --> 00:20:28,740
He wouldn't say, well, you know, that's tough.

252
00:20:28,940 --> 00:20:31,020
You bought the stock and you know what the risks were.

253
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:32,160
I'm done with it.

254
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:33,060
No, he wouldn't.

255
00:20:33,061 --> 00:20:36,140
He would say, I sold you that security and I thought it was good.

256
00:20:36,180 --> 00:20:37,740
It turns out it's not so good now.

257
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:40,960
So I'm going to get involved in that railroad and I'm going to try to make it good.

258
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:48,580
Morgan controlled 341 seats on the boards of more than 100 different companies.

259
00:20:49,900 --> 00:20:52,860
Investors respected Morgan, but the public did not.

260
00:20:52,861 --> 00:20:58,200
His railroad monopoly raised shipping prices, hurting farmers.

261
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:02,680
Morgan supported child labor and opposed labor unions.

262
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:09,240
President Teddy Roosevelt used antitrust regulations to bust up parts of Morgan's empire.

263
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:16,180
But when Morgan died in 1913, he received a funeral befitting royalty.

264
00:21:17,380 --> 00:21:22,760
The stock exchange closed for two hours as his hearse passed by.

265
00:21:22,860 --> 00:21:25,512
His death was treated like the death of a monarch.

266
00:21:25,513 --> 00:21:27,580
In other words, big funeral procession,

267
00:21:27,581 --> 00:21:32,220
front page news all over the world, editorials, one editorial saying he was a monster, thank

268
00:21:32,221 --> 00:21:32,921
God he's dead.

269
00:21:32,922 --> 00:21:36,140
Another editorial saying the great age has passed and we have to find someone

270
00:21:36,141 --> 00:21:40,409
like him around. The only trouble was there was no one like Morgan.

271
00:21:40,410 --> 00:21:41,620
He was sui generis,

272
00:21:41,621 --> 00:21:45,180
he was just by himself.

273
00:21:45,181 --> 00:21:50,980
Some hated Morgan so deeply that his company became the target of a terrorist bomb seven

274
00:21:50,981 --> 00:21:53,117
years after his death.

275
00:21:53,118 --> 00:21:59,140
On September 20th, 1920, a wagon loaded with explosives killed

276
00:21:59,141 --> 00:22:01,152
30 people and injured 100.

277
00:22:01,153 --> 00:22:05,640
The entrances to the Morgan Company and the New York Stock

278
00:22:05,641 --> 00:22:13,820
Exchange across the street were littered with bodies. The crime was never solved.

279
00:22:13,821 --> 00:22:20,460
wall street quickly recovered from the 1920 blast

280
00:22:20,461 --> 00:22:26,100
but more shocks were ahead the biggest boom market in history was coming

281
00:22:26,101 --> 00:22:28,980
so was the biggest crash

282
00:22:28,981 --> 00:22:37,920
in the early days of wall street merchants kept an actual inventory of securities on hand

283
00:22:37,921 --> 00:22:41,440
which they sold over the counter like any other product

284
00:22:41,441 --> 00:22:45,680
The Stock Exchange will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

285
00:22:46,700 --> 00:22:50,440
We now return to The Stock Exchange on Modern Marvels.

286
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:59,440
America had never seen anything like the 1920s.

287
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:02,740
World War I ended in victory.

288
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:05,960
Factories were booming.

289
00:23:06,800 --> 00:23:08,480
Families had money to burn.

290
00:23:08,481 --> 00:23:14,525
They go to the movies. They had a phonograph. The family might have had a car.

291
00:23:14,526 --> 00:23:15,300
All things

292
00:23:15,301 --> 00:23:17,800
are changing and getting better, and getting much better.

293
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:25,560
By 1920, the New York Stock Exchange, a private institution, looked more impressive than many

294
00:23:25,561 --> 00:23:26,941
government agencies.

295
00:23:26,942 --> 00:23:31,500
The Exchange had torn down its building at the turn of the century

296
00:23:31,501 --> 00:23:35,760
to construct a larger one that projected an image of strength.

297
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:40,120
The new trading floor was enormous.

298
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:48,940
Each stock was now traded at a particular spot called a post.

299
00:23:50,040 --> 00:23:53,460
Steel stocks were clustered at one post, railroads at another.

300
00:23:54,880 --> 00:23:57,940
Auctioneers called specialists controlled the bidding.

301
00:24:01,500 --> 00:24:08,140
When a sale was made, clerks would rush details of the transaction by pneumatic tube to the ticker tape room,

302
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:11,720
where typists would relay the news to the world.

303
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:19,760
In the 1920s, the exchange was a place of glamour and wonder.

304
00:24:20,820 --> 00:24:24,180
I started on the floor of the New York Stock Exchange as a page.

305
00:24:24,181 --> 00:24:29,040
in 1926, having just graduated from prep school,

306
00:24:29,660 --> 00:24:31,960
and I was 16 years old at that time.

307
00:24:32,600 --> 00:24:35,780
It was a very exuberant period.

308
00:24:36,740 --> 00:24:39,040
I thought it was kind of lively and a lot of fun.

309
00:24:41,180 --> 00:24:43,800
Stocks were becoming a national pastime.

310
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:46,840
Americans bought millions of radios

311
00:24:46,841 --> 00:24:49,200
and shares in the company that made them.

312
00:24:50,640 --> 00:24:53,800
As cars became popular, so did auto stocks.

313
00:24:54,180 --> 00:24:58,540
The demand for stocks pushed prices through the roof.

314
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:06,980
Between 1924 and 1929, the Dow Jones Industrial Average shot up more than 300%.

315
00:25:06,981 --> 00:25:11,260
You had a lot of people in the market that knew nothing about the market

316
00:25:11,261 --> 00:25:13,580
except they were going to make some quick money.

317
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:17,300
And the thing was obviously overblown.

318
00:25:17,301 --> 00:25:25,540
unscrupulous brokers made things worse by pressuring investors into buying questionable stocks

319
00:25:25,541 --> 00:25:33,300
there was a lot of opportunity for people to believe in what they were being told and there

320
00:25:33,301 --> 00:25:38,140
were a lot of people there willing to take advantage of telling them that this company

321
00:25:38,141 --> 00:25:45,640
or that stock or something was going to just really go gangbusters even more dangerous many

322
00:25:45,641 --> 00:25:51,305
investors bought stock on credit, known in the trade as buying on margin.

323
00:25:51,306 --> 00:25:52,780
You could buy stock

324
00:25:52,781 --> 00:25:55,679
if you were a good customer for 10% margin.

325
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:58,780
So if you wanted to buy a share of $100 stock,

326
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:03,400
you could put up $10, and the stock itself became collateral for a loan for the other $90.

327
00:26:04,980 --> 00:26:09,600
The widespread use of credit and the tremendous rise in stock prices

328
00:26:09,601 --> 00:26:13,900
made some investors wonder how long the good times could last.

329
00:26:14,820 --> 00:26:19,560
In 1928, stockbroker Charles Merrill of the firm Merrill Lynch

330
00:26:19,561 --> 00:26:22,540
sent a bluntly worded warning to clients.

331
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,140
Now is a good time to get out of debt.

332
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:29,380
We do not urge that you sell securities indiscriminately,

333
00:26:29,381 --> 00:26:33,120
but we do advise in no uncertain terms

334
00:26:33,121 --> 00:26:35,800
that you take advantage of present high prices

335
00:26:35,801 --> 00:26:38,840
and put your own financial house in order.

336
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:40,760
Charles E. Merrill.

337
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:54,900
Disaster struck Wall Street in October of 1929.

338
00:26:56,500 --> 00:26:59,460
Consumer spending on big-ticket items hit a slump,

339
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:02,400
causing several key stocks to decline.

340
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:06,880
The drop sparked a rash of margin calls,

341
00:27:06,881 --> 00:27:12,480
where brokers demanded that investors put more cash into their stock market accounts.

342
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:17,560
This was the risk of buying stock on credit.

343
00:27:18,460 --> 00:27:23,720
When a stock shrinks in price, it's no longer valuable enough to be collateral for the loan.

344
00:27:25,160 --> 00:27:28,940
Investors must put up cash or margin to even the scales.

345
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:32,480
If they don't, their account will be liquidated.

346
00:27:32,481 --> 00:27:38,080
October 24th, 1929

347
00:27:38,081 --> 00:27:42,360
Thousands of investors failed to come up with the necessary cash

348
00:27:42,361 --> 00:27:44,820
By the time their brokers entered the exchange

349
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:47,880
When the opening bell rang at 10 a.m.

350
00:27:48,560 --> 00:27:50,960
The liquidation sale began

351
00:27:50,961 --> 00:27:55,180
Well, it started off, as I recall, like a busy day

352
00:27:55,181 --> 00:27:57,620
And pretty soon it got worse and worse

353
00:27:57,621 --> 00:28:02,100
Suddenly, it appeared that

354
00:28:02,101 --> 00:28:08,380
Everyone wanted to sell and no one wanted to buy and there was a sense of frenzy.

355
00:28:09,780 --> 00:28:15,480
The credit binge that had built up the market was suddenly eating through it like a virus.

356
00:28:16,300 --> 00:28:21,120
The imbalance between sellers and buyers pushed all stock prices lower,

357
00:28:22,140 --> 00:28:26,300
forcing margin calls on other investors and more liquidation.

358
00:28:26,301 --> 00:28:33,160
so many shares were sold so quickly that the ticker was running four hours late

359
00:28:33,161 --> 00:28:38,540
thousands of investors flooded the financial district desperate for news

360
00:28:38,541 --> 00:28:46,640
and suddenly there was hope richard whitney vice president of the exchange met with the

361
00:28:46,641 --> 00:28:54,700
nation's top bankers and marched onto the trading floor and he walks up to u.s steel and says what's

362
00:28:54,701 --> 00:28:59,157
steel at, and he says 200, I'll buy 10,000 shares of steel at 200.

363
00:28:59,158 --> 00:29:00,440
And a roar goes up.

364
00:29:00,820 --> 00:29:02,860
The big guys are coming in. They're saving us.

365
00:29:04,820 --> 00:29:09,717
Whitney bought $20 million worth of stock in a manner of minutes.

366
00:29:09,718 --> 00:29:11,600
It was a powerful symbol.

367
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:14,525
Recovery, resistance, hope.

368
00:29:14,526 --> 00:29:20,280
Don't forget these people are seeing the whole place fall to pieces.

369
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:24,300
And here's a guy who steps up and starts showing courage.

370
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:30,020
It turned out Richard Whitney was not trying to save the market,

371
00:29:30,180 --> 00:29:34,400
but to fool it so the banks could eventually sell out at a better price.

372
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:39,420
His triumphant stand stopped the panic temporarily,

373
00:29:39,421 --> 00:29:43,380
but the downward spiral resumed the following week with a bigger crash

374
00:29:43,381 --> 00:29:47,060
and continued for the next three years.

375
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:50,200
The drop was staggering.

376
00:29:50,980 --> 00:29:54,400
General Electric went for more than $1,600 per share

377
00:29:54,401 --> 00:29:56,160
to $154.

378
00:29:57,180 --> 00:29:58,480
General Motors,

379
00:29:58,980 --> 00:30:00,320
$1,075

380
00:30:00,321 --> 00:30:01,760
to $40.

381
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:04,780
The Dow Jones Industrial Average

382
00:30:04,781 --> 00:30:06,560
fell 89%.

383
00:30:06,561 --> 00:30:08,780
$72 billion

384
00:30:08,781 --> 00:30:10,100
in investments

385
00:30:10,101 --> 00:30:11,620
wiped out.

386
00:30:12,440 --> 00:30:14,920
And people who had their whole life savings tied up on Wall Street

387
00:30:14,921 --> 00:30:16,860
vanished. It's out. No more.

388
00:30:17,320 --> 00:30:18,020
Zero.

389
00:30:18,360 --> 00:30:20,340
A person went from $500

390
00:30:20,341 --> 00:30:22,840
to $15,000 and now he's down

391
00:30:22,841 --> 00:30:23,541
to zero.

392
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:31,400
Economists contend the crash alone didn't cause the Great Depression that followed.

393
00:30:31,401 --> 00:30:34,400
But the public blamed Wall Street.

394
00:30:34,401 --> 00:30:41,400
The panic frightened people, and as they stopped spending money, the economy ground to a halt.

395
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:51,400
The crash revealed major flaws in America's unregulated market price for stocks.

396
00:30:51,401 --> 00:30:59,180
Now with the nation in ruins, the federal government was about to impose radical changes in the way the exchange did business.

397
00:31:01,640 --> 00:31:06,560
The stock market crash sparked the depression, but the causes were much broader.

398
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:13,960
They included distressed key industries, an end to the building boom, and bad loans leading to massive bank failures.

399
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:18,600
The stock exchange will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

400
00:31:18,601 --> 00:31:25,600
We now return to the Stock Exchange on Modern Marvels.

401
00:31:25,601 --> 00:31:28,600
This great nation will endure...

402
00:31:28,601 --> 00:31:33,600
Franklin Roosevelt was elected president in 1932 as a reformer.

403
00:31:33,601 --> 00:31:38,600
In his very first speech in office, he took aim at the stock market.

404
00:31:38,601 --> 00:31:45,600
There must be a strict supervision of all banking and credits and investments.

405
00:31:45,601 --> 00:31:55,360
There must be an end to speculation with other people's money.

406
00:31:57,460 --> 00:32:03,660
On his second day in office, the president ordered the New York Stock Exchange to close for a week.

407
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:10,080
He then pushed through Congress the most sweeping set of financial reforms ever enacted.

408
00:32:11,320 --> 00:32:13,800
Banks could no longer gamble on stocks.

409
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:17,060
brokers must act responsibly

410
00:32:17,061 --> 00:32:18,720
treating their customers money

411
00:32:18,721 --> 00:32:20,540
as if it were their own

412
00:32:20,541 --> 00:32:23,020
and corporations that offered

413
00:32:23,021 --> 00:32:25,220
stock to the public must file

414
00:32:25,221 --> 00:32:26,920
annual financial reports

415
00:32:26,921 --> 00:32:27,760
with the government

416
00:32:27,761 --> 00:32:31,200
the amount of information people

417
00:32:31,201 --> 00:32:32,620
had went up a great deal

418
00:32:32,621 --> 00:32:34,920
and I think eventually that got

419
00:32:34,921 --> 00:32:36,840
institutions and individual investors

420
00:32:36,841 --> 00:32:38,940
saying well we want to buy stocks now

421
00:32:38,941 --> 00:32:41,020
because the deck isn't stacked

422
00:32:41,021 --> 00:32:42,980
against us you know we really have a lot

423
00:32:42,981 --> 00:32:44,580
better information than we used to.

424
00:32:45,080 --> 00:32:46,780
And so now Wall Street isn't the

425
00:32:46,781 --> 00:32:49,160
den of thieves that we used to think

426
00:32:49,161 --> 00:32:51,160
it was. It's more of an honest place.

427
00:32:53,980 --> 00:32:55,080
Richard Whitney,

428
00:32:55,340 --> 00:32:57,200
now president of the exchange,

429
00:32:57,340 --> 00:32:59,020
spoke against the new regulations

430
00:32:59,021 --> 00:33:01,260
saying the market could police itself.

431
00:33:02,740 --> 00:33:05,000
But nothing could stop the president's

432
00:33:05,001 --> 00:33:06,700
push for government oversight.

433
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:09,080
Roosevelt created

434
00:33:09,081 --> 00:33:11,040
the Securities and Exchange Commission,

435
00:33:11,041 --> 00:33:11,840
the SEC,

436
00:33:11,841 --> 00:33:14,320
to enforce the new rules.

437
00:33:15,180 --> 00:33:17,280
Its first chairman was Joseph Kennedy.

438
00:33:19,440 --> 00:33:22,920
The SEC would indict more than 300 people

439
00:33:22,921 --> 00:33:24,840
in an effort to clean up Wall Street,

440
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:28,100
although the agency found it virtually impossible

441
00:33:28,101 --> 00:33:29,300
to win convictions.

442
00:33:30,560 --> 00:33:33,280
The only major figure to go to jail

443
00:33:33,281 --> 00:33:35,000
was Richard Whitney himself,

444
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:37,100
convicted of embezzlement.

445
00:33:38,120 --> 00:33:41,080
The man who led the market through its deepest crisis

446
00:33:41,081 --> 00:33:44,780
spent three years in Sing Sing before being released.

447
00:33:47,940 --> 00:33:53,540
The newly reformed market was a sleepy place in the 1930s and 40s

448
00:33:53,541 --> 00:33:55,800
as a weary public stayed away.

449
00:33:56,760 --> 00:34:01,300
Thousands of Wall Street workers lost faith in the paper chase and quit.

450
00:34:01,301 --> 00:34:04,940
You really didn't believe in paper anymore.

451
00:34:04,941 --> 00:34:11,980
you wanted to go out and earn your living doing something a lot more concrete.

452
00:34:13,560 --> 00:34:17,520
During World War II, the federal government, not the stock market,

453
00:34:17,640 --> 00:34:21,880
generated most of the money needed to reinvigorate American industry.

454
00:34:22,940 --> 00:34:26,080
The market generated less than 20%.

455
00:34:26,081 --> 00:34:30,680
The war did bring a historic milestone to Wall Street.

456
00:34:30,681 --> 00:34:36,900
women who had worked in the back rooms now appeared on the stock exchange floor

457
00:34:36,901 --> 00:34:41,960
ending a male-only tradition that had lasted 150 years.

458
00:34:44,660 --> 00:34:49,580
With victory came the market's most unique wartime contribution

459
00:34:49,581 --> 00:34:54,820
the mountain of ticker tape confetti that welcomed America's troops home.

460
00:34:54,821 --> 00:35:02,520
The baby boom years after World War II

461
00:35:02,521 --> 00:35:06,000
sparked another major expansion of the American economy

462
00:35:06,001 --> 00:35:10,560
But unlike the 1920s

463
00:35:10,561 --> 00:35:13,520
this boom was fueled by solid investing

464
00:35:13,521 --> 00:35:15,280
instead of speculation

465
00:35:15,281 --> 00:35:20,660
Leading the charge was Charles E. Merrill

466
00:35:20,661 --> 00:35:23,660
who opened hundreds of new Merrill Lynch offices

467
00:35:23,661 --> 00:35:24,760
in the suburbs

468
00:35:24,761 --> 00:35:28,760
The whole atmosphere changes.

469
00:35:29,960 --> 00:35:33,480
The brokerage house in the past was either for the sleazy characters or the bigwigs.

470
00:35:33,960 --> 00:35:35,180
Now it's middle class Americans.

471
00:35:35,940 --> 00:35:42,160
And Merrill says the person we're after is the GI, the ex-GI, with a wife and three kids and a Chevy.

472
00:35:42,840 --> 00:35:44,480
And that person should own shares.

473
00:35:45,360 --> 00:35:46,460
Own your share of America.

474
00:35:47,200 --> 00:35:47,900
And it works.

475
00:35:49,380 --> 00:35:53,160
Merrill Lynch offered investment classes for women

476
00:35:53,161 --> 00:35:57,760
and placed a how-to-invest exhibit in New York's Grand Central Station.

477
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:01,820
Commuters could drop in to see if stocks were right for them.

478
00:36:03,220 --> 00:36:06,760
One of their innovations was to make research reports,

479
00:36:06,761 --> 00:36:10,480
which they gave out free to clients and would-be clients,

480
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:12,820
saying, here, you know, here's what our company thinks,

481
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:16,720
it's investigated this company, and here's how we see its prospects.

482
00:36:19,640 --> 00:36:22,960
As American investors returned to stocks,

483
00:36:23,160 --> 00:36:24,880
the market finally recovered.

484
00:36:25,480 --> 00:36:26,240
90, probably.

485
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:27,180
90, probably.

486
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:28,540
How's it quarter?

487
00:36:28,880 --> 00:36:29,880
90 to a quarter.

488
00:36:30,100 --> 00:36:30,800
301.

489
00:36:31,060 --> 00:36:31,760
I need quarter.

490
00:36:31,800 --> 00:36:32,500
Take 100.

491
00:36:32,501 --> 00:36:37,380
In 1954, the Dow Jones Industrial Average broke 300.

492
00:36:38,040 --> 00:36:43,180
The market had set 25 years earlier, just before the 1929 crash.

493
00:36:45,520 --> 00:36:49,800
One of the biggest innovations in the 50s came not from Wall Street,

494
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:53,040
but from a university campus 800 miles away.

495
00:36:53,160 --> 00:37:00,940
Economist Harry Markowitz of the University of Chicago developed the theory of stock diversification.

496
00:37:02,440 --> 00:37:10,020
Investors, he said, should buy a wide range of stocks to reduce the risk of bankruptcy when a single stock goes bad.

497
00:37:13,300 --> 00:37:19,360
What Markowitz was saying is put your money into lots of different things, and on average you're going to do pretty well.

498
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:22,840
Even though you're never going to be at the top, you're never going to be at the bottom.

499
00:37:23,160 --> 00:37:30,160
The concept of a diversified stock portfolio is a basic tenant of modern investment.

500
00:37:30,920 --> 00:37:38,860
But for the 50s, the idea was so radical that it eventually earned Harry Markowitz the Nobel Prize for Economics.

501
00:37:41,820 --> 00:37:46,180
In the decades that followed, there were downturns in the market.

502
00:37:46,420 --> 00:37:52,300
In the 1960s, there were major slumps prompted by bad news during the Kennedy years.

503
00:37:53,160 --> 00:37:55,460
and setbacks in the Vietnam War.

504
00:37:56,200 --> 00:38:00,320
The OPEC oil embargo hurt stock prices in the 1970s

505
00:38:00,321 --> 00:38:03,600
as Americans waited in line for a gallon of gas.

506
00:38:04,600 --> 00:38:09,900
But the most dramatic event since the 29 crash was coming soon,

507
00:38:10,340 --> 00:38:12,680
the introduction of the computer.

508
00:38:16,680 --> 00:38:22,440
The price of a membership seat on the New York Stock Exchange in 1817 was $25.

509
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:25,260
Today, it is over $1 million.

510
00:38:26,160 --> 00:38:27,880
Modern Marvels will return.

511
00:38:29,960 --> 00:38:34,580
We now return to the Stock Exchange on Modern Marvels.

512
00:38:35,540 --> 00:38:39,660
A different kind of panic hit Wall Street in the 1960s.

513
00:38:40,120 --> 00:38:41,320
A paper panic.

514
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:49,300
The rise of pension plans and mutual funds pushed the trading volume to 11 million shares per day.

515
00:38:49,301 --> 00:38:58,900
every transaction was still processed by hand so you'd have piles and piles of these papers

516
00:38:58,901 --> 00:39:05,320
on desks and you had a lot of these clerks just moving these papers back and forth it's not

517
00:39:05,321 --> 00:39:11,800
surprising that when you had a lot of volume you had a crisis for brokerage companies the

518
00:39:11,801 --> 00:39:18,880
paper crunch was terrifying exhausted clerks were unable to balance the books each night

519
00:39:19,300 --> 00:39:25,600
The New York Stock Exchange had to close on Wednesdays to give clerks a chance to catch up.

520
00:39:25,601 --> 00:39:31,480
The clerks would work around the clock, and every once in a while they'd go in a cot and sleep for an hour and come right back again.

521
00:39:32,500 --> 00:39:33,700
They wouldn't go home for weeks.

522
00:39:34,640 --> 00:39:40,780
At its height in 1968, the paper crunch touched off a devastating cash flow crisis,

523
00:39:41,140 --> 00:39:45,180
which forced nearly a hundred brokerage companies into bankruptcy.

524
00:39:45,181 --> 00:39:53,880
Computers solve the problem with their ability to document stock transactions at the speed of light.

525
00:39:54,880 --> 00:40:01,280
Since the 1970s, computers have assisted brokers with every aspect of the securities business.

526
00:40:02,860 --> 00:40:11,440
They route smaller transactions directly to the trading posts, ending the delay caused by hand-carrying an order across the floor.

527
00:40:11,441 --> 00:40:19,800
computers have vastly improved the market's ability to handle the ever-growing volume of trades

528
00:40:19,801 --> 00:40:25,640
computers though have had a dark side

529
00:40:25,641 --> 00:40:31,480
they were largely responsible for the biggest single day drop in stock market history

530
00:40:31,481 --> 00:40:34,860
October 19 1987

531
00:40:34,861 --> 00:40:38,980
the market began a decline that raced out of control

532
00:40:41,440 --> 00:40:46,420
Lightning-fast computers had been pre-programmed to rapidly sell stocks

533
00:40:46,421 --> 00:40:50,440
when prices hit a predetermined level.

534
00:40:50,441 --> 00:40:52,440
Just press the button and you're sold.

535
00:40:52,441 --> 00:40:57,440
Which set off something at another computer, and a third computer, and a fourth computer.

536
00:40:57,441 --> 00:41:00,440
Now what happened was the volume was tremendous.

537
00:41:00,441 --> 00:41:02,440
People couldn't keep up with these things.

538
00:41:02,441 --> 00:41:09,440
The Dow Jones average plunged 508 points, a drop of 23%.

539
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:14,120
The plummet taught traders a lesson.

540
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:18,860
The New York Stock Exchange has installed circuit breaker programs

541
00:41:18,861 --> 00:41:22,280
that restrict trading when the Dow fluctuates too rapidly

542
00:41:22,281 --> 00:41:25,220
to prevent runaway disasters in the future.

543
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:38,560
This computer in Connecticut has brought the marketplace for stocks

544
00:41:38,561 --> 00:41:41,000
to an historic crossroads.

545
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:49,060
This is NASDAQ, built in 1971 by the National Association of Securities Dealers.

546
00:41:50,020 --> 00:41:57,280
The NASDAQ system is a quick and inexpensive way to trade without the need for face-to-face encounters.

547
00:41:59,980 --> 00:42:03,600
NASDAQ links hundreds of brokerage houses worldwide.

548
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:10,660
What we're doing is we're replacing the people on the floor, congregating in a group, okay,

549
00:42:10,661 --> 00:42:21,600
to various people in different houses on the street at their desks bidding and offering in this box.

550
00:42:21,960 --> 00:42:26,740
And everyone has access to what is going on at the same time.

551
00:42:27,580 --> 00:42:32,420
The NASDAQ interactive screen displays two columns for every stock.

552
00:42:32,900 --> 00:42:38,000
A list of brokers trying to buy shares for their clients along with the price they're willing to pay.

553
00:42:38,001 --> 00:42:42,340
and brokers trying to sell stock in the price they're willing to accept.

554
00:42:45,740 --> 00:42:51,520
NASDAQ primarily handles stock in America's younger, smaller, high-technology companies.

555
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:56,720
But NASDAQ's phenomenal success is prompting some observers

556
00:42:56,721 --> 00:43:00,100
to wonder about the future of the New York Stock Exchange,

557
00:43:00,560 --> 00:43:03,920
where shares of larger, more established corporations

558
00:43:03,921 --> 00:43:06,440
are bought and sold in a traditional auction.

559
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:14,280
Some economists think the days of face-to-face trading are numbered.

560
00:43:14,860 --> 00:43:17,160
We're always going to need a centralized place.

561
00:43:17,240 --> 00:43:21,480
The question is, does it have to be a physical location on Wall Street?

562
00:43:23,080 --> 00:43:26,260
You know, it seems to me that technology is changing now

563
00:43:26,261 --> 00:43:32,680
so that we can have a centralized market just by looking on our computer screens.

564
00:43:32,681 --> 00:43:41,340
Officials at the New York Stock Exchange maintain there are advantages to their time-tested way of doing business.

565
00:43:42,220 --> 00:43:50,980
Most important is the role of the 450 specialists on the floor who act as referees between the 900 brokers

566
00:43:50,981 --> 00:43:58,600
and sometimes buy and sell stock on their own to ensure the market keeps functioning during periods of crisis.

567
00:43:58,601 --> 00:44:08,160
whether the future belongs to computers or traditional traders or a combination of the two

568
00:44:08,161 --> 00:44:13,940
stocks seem destined to continue their central role in the global economy

569
00:44:13,941 --> 00:44:19,700
stock trends now circle the globe 24 hours a day

570
00:44:19,701 --> 00:44:25,980
from the united states to tokyo to europe and back to america again

571
00:44:25,981 --> 00:44:33,380
In the U.S., stocks generate tens of billions of dollars of new capital every year.

572
00:44:34,620 --> 00:44:39,360
Money to get new companies started and help older ones to expand.

573
00:44:40,700 --> 00:44:46,680
The Stock Exchange is the place where the capital comes from to make capitalism work.

